## Things you should know

- **Length is tight.** The first pass ran 5,883 words. To get under the ceiling I cut the second Karp story and parable, Karp's New Hampshire and brother remarks, "weeks of revolt", the Torah sentence and Stern's sincerity verdict. A repair pass has about 40 words of room.
- **Some "fixed" findings are only partly met to the letter.** The resolution says so where it applies, for example the lawsuit sits in the Type 8 section rather than the deference section, and the Type 1 test sits later than the reviewer asked.
- **I kept the record at ready/medium and did not flag a major thesis change.** The type and rival are unchanged and the rebuilt argument follows the reviewers' own prescriptions. The critic's position was that if no discriminator survives, the honest outcome is a research hold. The verifier may disagree with my call.
- **Bloomberg was read through a third-party reader**, because the site returned 403. That access route is disclosed in the record, and rereading it on bloomberg.com before release is carried as a research task.
- **The Pentagon review status rests on a thin check.** One web search found no report of a release, but its newest results were from July 2026; the stronger basis is his own October 11 "wait for the final report".
- **Still unverified:** no human has listened to any quotation; "dictator", "my wife" and "my daughter" rest on auto-captions alone; Sankar's own answer in the i2 case sits behind PACER and was not read.

